# Feature: [ietf-inet-types]: Domain Name and Host Data Types

## Parent Epic
- [ ] #24 - [ietf-inet-types: Common Internet Data Types](https://github.com/gintatkinson/3dgs-037/blob/main/docs/epics/epic-02-ietf-inet-types.md) (Parent Epic specification)

## Description
This feature specifies domain name, host, and Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) data types defined in the `ietf-inet-types` module (RFC 6021 / RFC 6991). These derived types provide standardized semantic representations, pattern constraints, length bounds, encoding rules, and normalization procedures for network identifiers and resources across management interfaces.

The specific typedefs covered by this feature specification are:
- **`domain-name`**: Represents a DNS domain name according to RFC 952, RFC 1034, RFC 1123, RFC 2782, and RFC 5890. It supports fully qualified domain names (FQDNs), service records (SRV), and root domain (`.`). Textual length is constrained to 1..253 characters (corresponding to the 255 octet DNS wire format). Encoding must be US-ASCII, using lowercase characters canonically, and internationalized domain names (IDNs) must use A-labels (punycode).
- **`host`**: Represents a network host identifier modeled as a YANG union of an IP address (`inet:ip-address`, combining IPv4 and IPv6 addresses with optional zone identifiers) or a DNS domain name (`inet:domain-name`).
- **`uri`**: Represents a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) as defined by STD 66 (RFC 3986). Requires US-ASCII encoding and mandatory RFC 3986 normalization (removal of unnecessary percent-encoding, lowercasing of scheme/host, and uppercasing of hexadecimal digits). Zero-length URIs are invalid and signify absent URIs.

